'''Work in progress.''' This is an unofficial downstream port. The device boots
postmarketOS, mounts its root filesystem and brings up USB networking, but
there is '''no usable graphical output''' and '''SSH sessions do not work'''
(see [[#Known issues]]). Do not expect a daily driver.

== Contributors ==
* (add yourself)

== Kernel ==
Downstream kernel 4.19.191, from the vendor kernel source release for this
device (branch <code>light-u-oss</code> of the official Xiaomi kernel source
repository).

Note the two marketing/engineering codenames: the device package uses
<code>thunder</code>, while the kernel defconfig and device tree use
<code>light</code>.

=== Required patches ===
Four fixes were needed beyond the usual downstream kernel cleanups (GCC
compatibility, SELinux host includes, prebuilt cpio replacement, VLA
initializers in the audio amplifier drivers).

==== 1. Device tree: use the SoC base DTS, not the device overlay ====
<code>light.dts</code> is a <code>/plugin/</code> overlay and is '''not''' a
bootable DTB. The base device tree is <code>mediatek/mt6833.dts</code>, which
is not listed in the MediaTek DTB Makefile and therefore never built.

Add it to <code>arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/Makefile</code>:
<pre>
dtb-$(CONFIG_ARCH_MEDIATEK) += mt6833.dtb
</pre>

Install the DTBs from the kernel APKBUILD's <code>package()</code>, since
<code>downstreamkernel_package</code> does not do it:
<pre>
make dtbs_install O="$_outdir" ARCH="$_carch" \
    INSTALL_DTBS_PATH="$pkgdir"/boot/dtbs
</pre>

and set <code>deviceinfo_dtb="mediatek/mt6833"</code>.

A good sanity check: the resulting <code>mt6833.dtb</code> is '''byte-for-byte
identical''' to the DTB extracted from the stock boot image (164815 bytes).

==== 2. The bootloader requires a compressed kernel ====
Symptom: bootloop with no USB enumeration at all, the bootloader retrying the
slot until it gives up.

The stock boot image carries a gzip-compressed kernel (magic <code>1f 8b</code>),
but <code>downstreamkernel_package</code> searches for
<code>zImage-dtb</code>, <code>Image.gz-dtb</code>, <code>*zImage</code>,
<code>Image</code> — '''<code>Image.gz</code> is not in that list''', so it
installs the raw <code>Image</code>. Fix it in <code>package()</code>:
<pre>
export KERNEL_IMAGE_NAME="Image.gz"
</pre>

==== 3. NULL dereference in u_serial (gs_close) ====
Symptom: kernel panic roughly 9 seconds into boot, in a <code>getty</code>
process, then reboot — a bootloop.

<pre>
Unable to handle kernel NULL pointer dereference at virtual address 170
[getty] gs_close+0x38/0x2b0
        tty_release+0x11c/0x670
        tty_open+0x144/0x4dc
</pre>

<code>gs_open()</code> in
<code>drivers/usb/gadget/function/u_serial.c</code> only assigns
<code>tty->driver_data</code> near its end, after several early error returns.
The tty layer still calls <code>gs_close()</code> on the release path of such a
failed open, and <code>gs_close()</code> dereferences the pointer without
checking. 0x170 is the offset of <code>port_lock</code> inside
<code>struct gs_port</code>.

<pre>
--- a/drivers/usb/gadget/function/u_serial.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/gadget/function/u_serial.c
@@ gs_close()
  struct gs_port *port = tty->driver_data;
  struct gserial *gser;

+ if (!port)
+     return;
+
  spin_lock_irq(&port->port_lock);
</pre>

Seven other functions in the same file also use
<code>tty->driver_data</code> without a NULL check; they were not on the
observed crash path but are candidates for similar panics.

==== 4. NULL dereference in the MediaTek DRM GEM ION import ====
Symptom: kernel panic about 10 seconds into boot, in the Wayland compositor
process, as soon as a graphical session starts.

<pre>
[compositor] mtk_gem_ion_import_dma_buf+0x58/0x160
             mtk_gem_prime_import+0x50/0x188
             drm_gem_prime_fd_to_handle+0xb4/0x1cc
             drm_ioctl
</pre>

In <code>drivers/gpu/drm/mediatek/mtk_drm_gem.c</code>,
<code>ion_import_dma_buf()</code> returns an ERR_PTR on failure, but
<code>handle->buffer</code> is dereferenced immediately afterwards — '''before'''
the <code>IS_ERR(handle)</code> check that the caller performs. ION is the
Android memory allocator and is not set up under postmarketOS, so the import
always fails.

Guard both the NULL ION client and the returned handle, and normalise NULL into
an error pointer (the caller only tests <code>IS_ERR</code>).

This stops the panic. It does '''not''' make the compositor work — the PRIME
import simply fails cleanly instead of killing the kernel.

== deviceinfo notes ==
Boot image parameters were taken from the stock boot image: header version 2,
page size 2048, base 0x00000000, kernel offset 0x40080000, ramdisk offset
0x51100000, tags and DTB offset 0x47c80000, cmdline
<code>bootopt=64S3,32N2,64N2 buildvariant=user</code>.

=== 4096-byte sectors ===
The UFS storage uses '''4096-byte logical sectors'''. Without accounting for
this, the initramfs fails with:
<pre>
Trying to mount subpartitions for 10 seconds...
ERROR: failed to mount subpartitions!
</pre>

<code>mount_subpartitions()</code> only probes a partition when
<code>fdisk</code> reports exactly two partitions on it. Read with the wrong
geometry, the nested GPT shows a single entry (the protective MBR), so it never
proceeds. Two changes are required, because the image must both be '''created'''
and '''read''' with the right geometry:

* <code>deviceinfo_rootfs_image_sector_size="4096"</code> (used by the initramfs for <code>losetup --sector-size</code>)
* <code>pmbootstrap install --sector-size 4096</code>

Verify by checking that the <code>EFI PART</code> signature sits at offset 4096
in the generated image rather than at 512.

Note that <code>boot_size</code> is effectively multiplied by 8 with this
geometry: a configured 60 MB yields a 480 MB boot partition.

== Installation ==
The device is A/B. <code>fastboot flash boot</code> resolves to the active slot
(<code>has-slot:boot</code> returns <code>yes</code>). The rootfs goes to
<code>userdata</code>, which is shared between slots.

Verified boot must be disabled or the custom boot image is rejected:
<pre>
avbtool make_vbmeta_image --flags 2 --padding_size 2048 --output vbmeta.img
fastboot flash vbmeta vbmeta.img
</pre>

Note that <code>fastboot --disable-verity --disable-verification flash</code>
fails with <code>Failed to find AVB_MAGIC at offset: 0</code>; the image
generated by avbtool already carries the flag, so plain
<code>fastboot flash</code> is what works.

== Known issues ==

=== No display output ===
The screen keeps whatever the bootloader drew. The kernel '''does''' drive the
panel: the initramfs on-screen keyboard renders through
<code>/dev/fb0</code>, so DRM registers and the framebuffer works.

Two separate problems:
* The kernel has <code>CONFIG_VT=y</code> but '''not''' <code>CONFIG_FRAMEBUFFER_CONSOLE</code>, so virtual terminals are never drawn. Enabling it '''breaks the boot''' — panic at about 1.3 s, before the initramfs, with no framebuffer console messages in the log. Cause not understood.
* Wayland compositors fail in the DRM PRIME import path described above.

=== SSH sessions do not work ===
Authentication succeeds and the session is fully set up — the server forks,
switches to the user and spawns the login shell — but '''no data flows in
either direction''', with or without a PTY. Both processes sit in ordinary
interruptible waits (<code>do_select</code>, <code>do_sys_poll</code>). The
server logs nothing after the successful authentication.

Ruled out by measurement: CPU load (98% idle), memory (3.4 GB free), disk
(2% used), missing shell or home directory, virtual console gettys, and the
graphical stack (it fails identically on a console-only image).

Two unrelated real defects were found and fixed along the way, neither of which
resolved this:
* <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> pointed at <code>127.0.0.53</code>, the systemd-resolved stub, on an OpenRC system where nothing listens there.
* <code>/dev/ptmx</code> was a standalone device node rather than a symlink to <code>pts/ptmx</code>, and devpts was mounted with <code>ptmxmode=000</code>.

A busybox <code>telnetd</code> started from <code>/etc/local.d</code> works
perfectly on the same device, which is what made it clear that PTYs, pipes and
sockets are all fine.

=== Load average without CPU usage ===
Load average settles around 15 while the CPU is 98% idle and no userspace
process is busy — kernel threads waiting on hardware that has no Android
services driving it. Harmless in practice, but it makes ping latency on the USB
link jump from 0.3 ms to roughly 90 ms, which is misleading when diagnosing.

=== Charging ===
Believed not to work. The device drains while running postmarketOS even when
connected over USB.

== Debugging notes ==

=== Read panics from pstore ===
The kernel has <code>CONFIG_PSTORE_RAM</code> and
<code>CONFIG_PSTORE_CONSOLE</code>, so the console log survives a reboot. After
a bootloop, boot with <code>pmos.debug-shell</code> on the kernel cmdline and
read <code>/sys/fs/pstore/console-ramoops-0</code>. This is how both kernel
panics above were found.

The cmdline is a fixed 512-byte field in the boot image header, so
<code>pmos.debug-shell</code> can be appended to an existing boot image without
repacking it.

=== The on-screen keyboard breaks the debug shell ===
In the initramfs debug shell, <code>buffyboard</code> competes for terminal
input and truncates typed lines erratically, anywhere from 32 down to 8
characters. Run <code>pkill buffyboard</code> once per boot before doing any
real work there.

=== pmbootstrap only adds packages ===
Changing <code>ui</code> does not remove the previous UI; packages are only
ever added to the existing rootfs. Use <code>pmbootstrap install --zap</code>
when removing things. Also note that apk runs <code>.post-install</code> only
on a fresh install — declare <code>.post-upgrade</code> as well, or
device-package fixups will silently stop being applied on rebuilds.

=== pmos_continue_boot breaks USB ===
Leaving the initramfs debug shell with <code>pmos_continue_boot</code> left the
device without USB enumeration twice. Flashing a normal boot image and
rebooting works. Use the debug shell for inspection, not for handing over to a
usable system.
